1
00:00:00,836 --> 00:00:09,369
Welcome to Egypt's regions, part 1 of 1. First, I clear the chosen names for a
fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:10,023 --> 00:00:20,600
We work through 27 of the Egypt regions the map names, once each: where it
sits, one fact worth keeping, and a hook to remember it by.

3
00:00:21,262 --> 00:00:23,514
Let's bring Damietta into view.

4
00:00:29,117 --> 00:00:40,460
Damietta stands on the Mediterranean coast at the mouth of the Damietta branch
of the Nile, at the north-east corner of the delta, with Dakahlia inland to
the south.

5
00:00:41,115 --> 00:00:51,309
The Fifth Crusade besieged this port for eighteen months and took it in 1219,
and Francis of Assisi crossed the siege lines here to meet the sultan.

6
00:00:51,964 --> 00:01:00,753
Remember the Delta port that held out for a year and a half against a crusade,
and that still builds most of the country’s furniture.

7
00:01:01,431 --> 00:01:03,730
Let's bring Kafr el-Sheikh into view.

8
00:01:09,487 --> 00:01:24,715
Kafr el-Sheikh covers the northern edge of the Delta between Gharbia to the
south-west and Dakahlia to the south-east, reaching the Mediterranean shore
between the two branches of the Nile, and its name means the sheikh’s village.

9
00:01:25,368 --> 00:01:36,792
Its Burullus lagoon is one of the great wetlands of the Delta and a stopping
point on the migration route between Europe and Africa, and almost all of it
is farmland of rice and cotton.

10
00:01:37,450 --> 00:01:45,844
Remember the sheikh’s village on the Delta’s northern shore, with the lagoon
where European and African birds meet.

11
00:01:46,533 --> 00:01:48,785
Let's bring Port Said into view.

12
00:01:54,500 --> 00:02:06,226
Port Said stands on the Mediterranean at the northern entrance of the Suez
Canal, on the narrow strip of land north of the waterway, with Al Sharqia to
the west and Sinai across the canal to the east.

13
00:02:06,889 --> 00:02:20,241
It was a free port for decades, so Egyptians came here to buy imported goods
when nowhere else in the country had them, and the Anglo-French invasion of
1956 landed on the same frontage.

14
00:02:20,897 --> 00:02:31,729
Remember the northern gate of the canal, the free port that stocked the whole
country, and the beach where the invasion of 1956 came ashore.

15
00:02:32,449 --> 00:02:34,620
Let's bring Dakahlia into view.

16
00:02:43,739 --> 00:02:55,848
Dakahlia fills the heart of the Delta, between Damietta to the north and
Qalyubia to the south, with the Damietta branch of the Nile running down
through it and Mansoura as its capital.

17
00:02:56,511 --> 00:03:09,259
Mansoura is where Louis IX of France was held prisoner in 1250, so his crusade
ended in a house in this town, and the governorate is one of the most
productive farming ones in the Delta.

18
00:03:09,913 --> 00:03:17,204
Remember the Delta governorate whose capital was a prison for a French king,
and whose name comes from a village nobody has heard of.

19
00:03:17,898 --> 00:03:20,023
Let's bring Gharbia into view.

20
00:03:26,602 --> 00:03:36,923
Gharbia is the western half of the Delta, lying between the Rosetta and
Damietta branches of the Nile and well inland from the sea, with Tanta in the
middle.

21
00:03:37,581 --> 00:03:52,256
Mahalla al-Kubra in it is Egypt’s textile town, and its mill strikes fed
straight into the uprising of 2011, while Tanta’s festival for al-Sayyid
al-Badawi draws crowds the size of the Hajj.

22
00:03:52,915 --> 00:04:01,530
Remember the western Delta, where the mills lit the uprising of 2011 and the
great festival is said to rival the Hajj.

23
00:04:02,227 --> 00:04:04,352
Let's bring Alexandria into view.

24
00:04:10,597 --> 00:04:22,625
Alexandria sits on the Mediterranean at the western edge of the Delta, with
Beheira inland to the east and Matrouh along the coast to the west, and the
city fills almost the whole governorate.

25
00:04:23,295 --> 00:04:37,877
Alexander the Great founded and named it in 331 BC and laid it out himself,
and its library was the largest in the ancient world, while its lighthouse has
long since toppled.

26
00:04:38,535 --> 00:04:46,128
Remember the city Alexander laid out on the Mediterranean shore, whose
lighthouse has fallen and whose royal quarter now lies under the harbour.

27
00:04:46,806 --> 00:04:49,825
Let's bring Al Sharqia into view.

28
00:04:54,507 --> 00:05:10,419
Al Sharqia is the eastern Delta, running from the edge of the Delta near Cairo
out towards Port Said and Ismailia, with Zagazig as its capital and the
Damietta branch of the Nile separating it from Dakahlia to the west.

29
00:05:11,069 --> 00:05:23,562
Bubastis here was the cult city of Bastet, the cat goddess, and Herodotus
called her festival the rowdiest in Egypt, and the governorate is the
country’s largest producer of oranges.

30
00:05:24,214 --> 00:05:33,723
Remember the eastern Delta, the cat goddess’s festival town, and the orange
groves that supply most of the country’s fruit.

31
00:05:34,411 --> 00:05:36,454
Let's bring Beheira into view.

32
00:05:42,887 --> 00:05:56,273
Beheira is the broad western Delta governorate on the Mediterranean between
Alexandria and Kafr el-Sheikh, reaching inland to the desert edge near Giza
and Matrouh, with Damanhur as its capital.

33
00:05:56,925 --> 00:06:08,140
Rashid, the old Rosetta, is here, and it was there in 1799 that a French
soldier found the Rosetta Stone, the key that unlocked hieroglyphs.

34
00:06:08,798 --> 00:06:16,426
Remember the wide green Delta governorate whose name means lake, holding the
stone that unlocked hieroglyphs.

35
00:06:17,158 --> 00:06:19,666
Let's bring Ismailia into view.

36
00:06:25,784 --> 00:06:35,722
Ismailia sits on the isthmus between the Mediterranean and the Gulf of Suez,
east of Cairo and north of Suez, with the canal running along its southern
edge.

37
00:06:36,383 --> 00:06:48,583
Lake Timsah, a natural lake in the middle of it, is the one the canal was
routed through, and the town was named for the khedive who built the canal and
bankrupted Egypt doing it.

38
00:06:49,235 --> 00:06:55,713
Remember the canal town with villas and wide avenues, and the natural lake the
canal had to cross.

39
00:06:56,400 --> 00:06:58,397
Let's bring Monufia into view.

40
00:07:03,690 --> 00:07:14,476
Monufia is a small Delta governorate inland to the south of Tanta, hemmed
between Gharbia, Qalyubia and Beheira, with Shibin el Kom as its capital.

41
00:07:15,130 --> 00:07:27,077
It has no desert at all, every square metre is Delta farmland, and two
Egyptian presidents, Sadat and Mubarak, were both born in this small
governorate.

42
00:07:27,728 --> 00:07:35,483
Remember the small all-farmland Delta governorate that produced two presidents
and has not a grain of desert in it.

43
00:07:36,184 --> 00:07:40,189
Let's bring North Sinai into view.

44
00:07:43,823 --> 00:07:56,664
North Sinai fills the flat northern half of the Sinai peninsula, from the
Mediterranean at El Arish down to the Gulf of Suez, facing the delta coast
across the water.

45
00:07:57,320 --> 00:08:13,376
The coastal road across it was the invasion route between Egypt and Asia for
four thousand years, walked by armies in both directions, and its Bedouin
tribes speak a dialect closer to the Levant than to the Nile.

46
00:08:14,033 --> 00:08:21,754
Remember the flat northern half of Sinai, the invasion road along its shore,
and the Bedouin dialects that lean towards the Levant.

47
00:08:22,435 --> 00:08:25,117
Let's bring Qalyubia into view.

48
00:08:30,831 --> 00:08:41,408
Qalyubia is the narrow governorate just north of Cairo, wrapping around the
apex of the Delta where the Nile splits, with Benha as its capital.

49
00:08:42,069 --> 00:08:54,399
The Delta Barrages here, begun in 1843, were the first great modern attempt to
control the Nile’s flow, and the governorate is now effectively Cairo’s
northern suburb.

50
00:08:55,059 --> 00:09:01,886
Remember the strip of farmland north of Cairo, where the Delta Barrages first
taught Egypt to hold back the river.

51
00:09:02,561 --> 00:09:04,604
Let's bring Cairo into view.

52
00:09:10,163 --> 00:09:21,332
Cairo sits at the apex of the Delta where the Nile divides, with no sea at
all, hemmed in by Giza across the river to the west and Qalyubia to the north.

53
00:09:21,996 --> 00:09:32,143
Al-Qahira means the victorious, and it was named in 969 for the planet Mars,
which was rising in the sky when the Fatimids laid the foundations.

54
00:09:32,793 --> 00:09:40,548
Remember the capital named for the rising planet Mars, standing exactly where
the river chooses to split.

55
00:09:41,244 --> 00:09:44,181
Let's bring Matrouh into view.

56
00:09:48,576 --> 00:10:02,136
Matrouh is the long north-western governorate, running from the Mediterranean
above Alexandria inland across the Libyan desert to the borders of Giza and
the New Valley, with Matruh on the coast.

57
00:10:02,800 --> 00:10:15,675
Egypt’s longest stretch of Mediterranean coast is here, with water clear
enough to look Caribbean, and El Alamein nearby holds about twelve thousand
war graves in three cemeteries.

58
00:10:16,341 --> 00:10:25,722
Remember the long desert coast above Alexandria, with the clearest sea in
Egypt and the battlefields of 1942 behind it.

59
00:10:26,501 --> 00:10:28,881
Let's bring Suez into view.

60
00:10:43,089 --> 00:10:55,837
Suez stands at the southern end of the canal where it opens into the Gulf of
Suez, north of the Red Sea governorate and south of Cairo, with desert on both
sides.

61
00:10:56,492 --> 00:11:11,806
The canal opened in 1869 and cut the voyage from London to Bombay by about
seven thousand kilometres, and it needs no locks because the Mediterranean and
the Red Sea sit at effectively the same level.

62
00:11:12,462 --> 00:11:19,196
Remember the lockless canal at the foot of the country, where the sea is at
the same height at both ends.

63
00:11:19,973 --> 00:11:23,119
Let's bring Faiyum into view.

64
00:11:36,628 --> 00:11:49,759
Faiyum is an oasis basin on the west side of the Nile valley, south of Giza
and north of Beni Suef, with the river fed into it by a channel dug in the
Middle Kingdom.

65
00:11:50,419 --> 00:12:03,213
Lake Qarun in it is a remnant of a far larger lake, and its Fayum mummy
portraits, painted on wood in the Roman period, are the most lifelike faces to
survive from the ancient world.

66
00:12:03,867 --> 00:12:13,074
Remember the green basin inside the desert that the Nile feeds by hand, and
the painted wooden faces staring out of its tombs.

67
00:12:13,832 --> 00:12:16,340
Let's bring Beni Suef into view.

68
00:12:30,836 --> 00:12:42,864
Beni Suef is a governorate of the middle Nile valley on the west bank of the
river, south of Faiyum and Giza and north of Minya, with desert closing in on
both sides.

69
00:12:43,516 --> 00:12:56,438
At Meidum here a pyramid partly collapsed, leaving a tower standing inside its
own rubble, which shows how the shape was worked out, and the governorate is a
major producer of cement.

70
00:12:57,087 --> 00:13:04,680
Remember the sons of Suef on the middle river, with the pyramid that fell down
on itself and left the answer in the rubble.

71
00:13:05,367 --> 00:13:07,108
Let's bring Giza into view.

72
00:13:13,266 --> 00:13:26,652
Giza is the great governorate on the west bank of the Nile opposite Cairo,
running from the Delta in the north down through the desert past the pyramids,
with Giza city at its heart.

73
00:13:27,307 --> 00:13:42,110
The Great Pyramid was the tallest building in the world for about 3,800 years,
longer than anything else has ever held the record, and the Sphinx beside it
is carved from a single ridge of limestone.

74
00:13:42,770 --> 00:13:50,444
Remember the west bank with the oldest tallest building on earth, and the
Sphinx cut from the quarry ridge that was left behind.

75
00:13:51,135 --> 00:13:54,247
Let's bring South Sinai into view.

76
00:13:59,667 --> 00:14:11,695
South Sinai is the mountainous southern half of the Sinai peninsula, between
the Gulf of Suez and the Gulf of Aqaba, with El Tur as its capital on the
shore.

77
00:14:12,357 --> 00:14:24,594
St Catherine’s monastery at the foot of Mount Sinai has been occupied
continuously since 565 and holds the second largest collection of early
manuscripts after the Vatican.

78
00:14:25,248 --> 00:14:32,493
Remember the mountain half of Sinai, with the oldest working monastery in the
world and the deep Blue Hole off its reefs.

79
00:14:33,182 --> 00:14:35,144
Let's bring Minya into view.

80
00:14:40,659 --> 00:14:52,002
Minya is in the middle of the Nile valley, north of Asyut and south of Beni
Suef, with the river running through it and the desert hills of the Red Sea
governorate close on the east.

81
00:14:52,657 --> 00:15:06,171
Amarna is here, the city Akhenaten built in a single reign for his one-god
religion and abandoned immediately after his death, and the governorate has
the largest Christian share of any in Egypt.

82
00:15:06,834 --> 00:15:14,891
Remember the bride of Upper Egypt, the most Christian of the governorates,
with the empty city of the one-god king on its edge.

83
00:15:15,564 --> 00:15:17,944
Let's bring Asyut into view.

84
00:15:23,024 --> 00:15:33,299
Asyut is on the middle Nile, north of Sohag and south of Minya, with the
valley narrow and the desert hills pressing close on the east.

85
00:15:33,958 --> 00:15:44,198
Its name comes from Ancient Egyptian Zawty through Coptic Siout, meaning
guardian, because it guarded the caravan route that ran forty days to Darfur.

86
00:15:44,854 --> 00:15:50,229
Remember the guardian town that watched the caravan road south for forty days
to Darfur.

87
00:15:50,911 --> 00:15:53,129
Let's bring Sohag into view.

88
00:15:58,361 --> 00:16:10,041
Sohag is on the middle Nile between Asyut to the north and Qena to the south,
with the river valley narrow and the western desert closing in beside it.

89
00:16:10,694 --> 00:16:24,939
Abydos here was the cult centre of Osiris and holds the king list that gave
Egyptologists their chronology, and the red and white monasteries nearby are
among the oldest Coptic buildings still standing.

90
00:16:25,602 --> 00:16:35,923
Remember the governorate with Osiris’s shrine and the king list that dated the
pharaohs, and the two fifth-century monasteries nearby.

91
00:16:36,649 --> 00:16:38,820
Let's bring Luxor into view.

92
00:16:45,224 --> 00:16:59,876
Luxor is a small governorate on the middle Nile, carved out of the old Qena
governorate and lying just to the north of it, holding the temples of Karnak
on the east bank and the tombs of the west bank opposite.

93
00:17:00,530 --> 00:17:13,661
The Greeks called it Thebes and the Arabs al-Uqsur, the palaces, and
Tutankhamun’s tomb was found here in 1922, the only royal tomb in the Valley
of the Kings discovered nearly intact.

94
00:17:14,316 --> 00:17:22,071
Remember the narrow strip of temples and tombs where the boy king’s tomb was
found, and the columns of Karnak.

95
00:17:22,801 --> 00:17:25,529
Let's bring Qena into view.

96
00:17:31,247 --> 00:17:42,927
Qena sits on the middle Nile between Sohag to the north and Luxor to the
south, with the river bending sharply east around the city and the Red Sea
governorate’s border to the east.

97
00:17:43,588 --> 00:17:57,241
The Qena bend is where the Nile swings sharply east, the sharpest turn in its
whole course, and Dendera’s temple of Hathor here had its ceiling zodiac
carved in the last years of the Ptolemies.

98
00:17:57,907 --> 00:18:05,628
Remember the sharpest bend in the Nile and the temple ceiling zodiac that
ended up in the Louvre.

99
00:18:06,309 --> 00:18:08,689
Let's bring Red Sea into view.

100
00:18:13,760 --> 00:18:24,975
The Red Sea governorate is the long eastern strip along the shore of the Red
Sea, running from the Gulf of Suez down past Hurghada to the border with
Sudan.

101
00:18:25,639 --> 00:18:37,237
The sea it takes its name from is one of the saltiest in the world and the
only one with no river flowing into it, and these reefs carry some of the
highest coral cover on earth.

102
00:18:37,890 --> 00:18:47,399
Remember the long eastern shore with the saltiest sea and no river at all, and
the reefs scientists are watching as the water warms.

103
00:18:48,073 --> 00:18:50,836
Let's bring New Valley into view.

104
00:18:55,571 --> 00:19:07,042
New Valley is the vast desert governorate of the far south-west, running from
the edge of the Nile valley near Qena and Asyut out to the Libyan border, with
the Kharga oasis in it.

105
00:19:07,696 --> 00:19:20,873
It is the largest governorate in Egypt, about forty-four per cent of the
country, with well under one per cent of its people, and its oases sit over
one of the largest bodies of fossil water on earth.

106
00:19:21,532 --> 00:19:29,845
Remember the giant empty governorate that is nearly half the country for less
than one per cent of the people.

107
00:19:30,518 --> 00:19:32,561
Let's bring Aswan into view.

108
00:19:37,738 --> 00:19:48,059
Aswan is the southernmost governorate on the Nile, below Qena and west of the
Red Sea, where the river runs through desert to the Nubian frontier.

109
00:19:48,721 --> 00:20:03,396
Eratosthenes measured the size of the Earth using a well here in about 240 BC
and got within a few per cent, and the High Dam of 1970 ended the Nile flood
and created Lake Nasser.

110
00:20:04,050 --> 00:20:11,167
Remember the southern frontier town where a well proved the size of the Earth,
and the dam that tamed the river’s flood.

111
00:20:17,392 --> 00:20:51,592
27 regions of Egypt, one for each target of the map: Damietta, Kafr el-Sheikh,
Port Said, Dakahlia, Gharbia, Alexandria, Al Sharqia, Beheira, Ismailia,
Monufia, North Sinai, Qalyubia, Cairo, Matrouh, Suez, Faiyum, Beni Suef, Giza,
South Sinai, Minya, Asyut, Sohag, Luxor, Qena, Red Sea, New Valley and Aswan.

112
00:20:52,257 --> 00:21:02,067
Remember the shape of Egypt from the inside: the regions nest around each
other, and every name on the map belongs somewhere in this one country.
